1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,960
The crab has evolved at
least five different times.

2
00:00:03,423 --> 00:00:05,543
Different groups of
crustaceans, each on its

3
00:00:05,773 --> 00:00:08,383
own branch, kept ending
up with the same body.

4
00:00:09,103 --> 00:00:10,643
A flat, wide shell.

5
00:00:11,223 --> 00:00:12,843
A tail tucked underneath.

6
00:00:13,571 --> 00:00:16,951
It happened so often that biologists
gave it a name. Carcinisation.

7
00:00:19,171 --> 00:00:21,151
And it's become a running joke.

8
00:00:21,771 --> 00:00:23,731
Nature keeps trying to make a crab.

9
00:00:24,275 --> 00:00:27,015
But behind the joke is a serious idea.

10
00:00:27,420 --> 00:00:30,580
Evolution doesn't have to
invent everything from scratch.

11
00:00:31,500 --> 00:00:35,480
When it faces the same problem,
it often finds the same answer.

12
00:00:36,315 --> 00:00:38,155
Look at the eye of an octopus.

13
00:00:38,795 --> 00:00:40,735
A lens, an iris, a retina.

14
00:00:40,815 --> 00:00:42,975
It looks a lot like yours.

15
00:00:43,554 --> 00:00:48,674
But our last common ancestor was a small,
worm-like animal that had no such eye.

16
00:00:49,574 --> 00:00:52,394
Octopuses and humans built
theirs independently.

17
00:00:52,995 --> 00:00:57,455
And by one estimate, eyes have evolved
on their own more than forty times.

18
00:00:58,189 --> 00:01:00,189
Flight evolved four times.

19
00:01:00,749 --> 00:01:03,769
In insects, in pterosaurs,
in birds and in bats.

20
00:01:04,338 --> 00:01:06,318
And look at these three. A shark.

21
00:01:06,548 --> 00:01:08,701
An ichthyosaur. A dolphin.

22
00:01:09,001 --> 00:01:11,261
A fish, a reptile and a mammal.

23
00:01:11,981 --> 00:01:15,781
The same shape, because water pushes
back on all of them the same way.

24
00:01:16,174 --> 00:01:19,514
It works on land too.
This is a wolf skull.

25
00:01:19,840 --> 00:01:22,760
And this is the skull
of a Tasmanian tiger.

26
00:01:23,520 --> 00:01:25,360
They look remarkably alike.

27
00:01:26,040 --> 00:01:28,700
But the Tasmanian tiger was a marsupial.

28
00:01:29,060 --> 00:01:30,860
It carried its young in a pouch.

29
00:01:31,599 --> 00:01:35,199
It was more closely related to
a kangaroo than to any wolf.

30
00:01:35,855 --> 00:01:38,195
This is called convergent evolution.

31
00:01:39,065 --> 00:01:42,645
By the way, if you like videos
like this, consider subscribing.

32
00:01:43,365 --> 00:01:44,945
It really helps the channel.

33
00:01:45,985 --> 00:01:47,705
Which raises a question.

34
00:01:48,345 --> 00:01:51,665
In another video, we asked
how strange aliens could be.

35
00:01:52,112 --> 00:01:56,432
But what if evolution on another planet
keeps finding the same answers too?

36
00:01:56,987 --> 00:01:59,567
Could an intelligent
alien end up looking...

37
00:01:59,647 --> 00:02:01,207
surprisingly familiar?

38
00:02:02,589 --> 00:02:04,629
Let's start with the simplest thing.

39
00:02:05,169 --> 00:02:07,349
You have a left side and a right side.

40
00:02:07,785 --> 00:02:09,185
That sounds trivial.

41
00:02:09,845 --> 00:02:13,985
But almost every animal that actively
moves around is built the same way.

42
00:02:14,324 --> 00:02:17,984
If you mostly move in one
direction, it pays to have a front.

43
00:02:18,644 --> 00:02:20,384
The front meets the world first.

44
00:02:20,464 --> 00:02:22,224
So that's where the senses go.

45
00:02:22,764 --> 00:02:24,364
Eyes, ears, a nose.

46
00:02:24,444 --> 00:02:26,044
Whatever the animal has.

47
00:02:26,378 --> 00:02:28,278
And it pays to have
the brain right next to

48
00:02:28,508 --> 00:02:31,298
them, so the signals don't have far to go.

49
00:02:31,593 --> 00:02:33,973
Biologists call this cephalisation.

50
00:02:34,053 --> 00:02:35,813
Building a head.

51
00:02:36,405 --> 00:02:40,005
You can see it in worms,
insects, octopuses and us.

52
00:02:40,619 --> 00:02:45,379
An alien that walks, swims or flies
through its world faces the same problem.

53
00:02:46,119 --> 00:02:48,159
And physics is the same there too.

54
00:02:50,619 --> 00:02:54,059
Now, the part that matters most
for a technological species.

55
00:02:54,824 --> 00:02:56,924
A dolphin has a large brain.

56
00:02:57,104 --> 00:02:59,544
It's clever, it's social, it learns.

57
00:02:59,955 --> 00:03:03,355
But a dolphin will never light
a fire, or forge a knife.

58
00:03:04,055 --> 00:03:06,155
It has no hands to make one with.

59
00:03:06,509 --> 00:03:08,009
Crows are clever too.

60
00:03:08,429 --> 00:03:10,469
Some of them carve twigs into hooks.

61
00:03:11,149 --> 00:03:13,809
But everything they make,
they make with their beak.

62
00:03:14,289 --> 00:03:15,709
Humans got lucky.

63
00:03:16,389 --> 00:03:20,329
Our legs carry us around, which leaves
our hands free to do everything else.

64
00:03:20,958 --> 00:03:23,478
Many researchers think
it worked like a loop.

65
00:03:23,718 --> 00:03:25,978
Better hands made better tools.

66
00:03:26,058 --> 00:03:28,298
Better tools rewarded better brains.

67
00:03:28,528 --> 00:03:31,478
And better brains made better tools again.

68
00:03:31,866 --> 00:03:35,846
So an alien that builds things
probably doesn't need hands like ours.

69
00:03:36,227 --> 00:03:39,127
It might have tentacles.
Or claws. Or a trunk.

70
00:03:39,407 --> 00:03:41,507
Or four legs and two arms.

71
00:03:41,849 --> 00:03:43,949
But it would probably need
something that moves it

72
00:03:44,179 --> 00:03:47,209
around, and something else
that handles the world.

73
00:03:48,212 --> 00:03:50,152
And even that isn't enough.

74
00:03:50,568 --> 00:03:55,468
An octopus has eight incredibly
skilful arms. It opens jars.

75
00:03:56,008 --> 00:03:57,908
It escapes from aquariums.

76
00:03:58,048 --> 00:04:02,768
But most octopuses survive only a year
or two, and they're mostly loners.

77
00:04:03,071 --> 00:04:05,771
Everything an octopus learns dies with it.

78
00:04:06,271 --> 00:04:08,051
Nobody passes it on.

79
00:04:08,651 --> 00:04:11,791
A technological species
needs hands and brains.

80
00:04:12,491 --> 00:04:15,311
But it also needs time,
and others to learn from.

81
00:04:17,901 --> 00:04:19,301
And now, the twist.

82
00:04:19,941 --> 00:04:23,361
There's an even bigger reason an
alien we meet might look familiar.

83
00:04:24,322 --> 00:04:26,882
We won't be meeting all
the life in the galaxy.

84
00:04:27,682 --> 00:04:29,742
Imagine a galaxy full of life.

85
00:04:29,822 --> 00:04:31,822
Countless microbes.

86
00:04:32,362 --> 00:04:34,582
Endless oceans of simple creatures.

87
00:04:34,662 --> 00:04:36,882
Maybe some clever animals.

88
00:04:37,340 --> 00:04:41,080
And just a handful of species
that build radios and spaceships.

89
00:04:41,403 --> 00:04:45,743
If we ever meet an alien civilisation,
it will come from that handful.

90
00:04:46,212 --> 00:04:49,432
We won't travel across the
galaxy to meet alien grass.

91
00:04:49,872 --> 00:04:52,232
Alien grass won't call us.

92
00:04:53,137 --> 00:04:55,697
We'll meet the ones that can
sense their surroundings,

93
00:04:55,917 --> 00:04:59,317
handle things precisely, work
together and build machines.

94
00:05:00,131 --> 00:05:03,691
In other words, the ones that
solved the same problems we did.

95
00:05:03,981 --> 00:05:07,901
That's a filter. And it doesn't
select for any random shape.

96
00:05:08,381 --> 00:05:10,081
It selects for a toolkit.

97
00:05:10,494 --> 00:05:12,954
That doesn't mean the
alien will look human.

98
00:05:13,514 --> 00:05:16,034
It means some of its
problems may look familiar.

99
00:05:18,664 --> 00:05:21,004
So what else might be in that toolkit?

100
00:05:21,504 --> 00:05:23,364
Probably some kind of vision.

101
00:05:24,184 --> 00:05:26,344
Wherever there's a star, light carries an

102
00:05:26,574 --> 00:05:29,104
enormous amount of
information about the world.

103
00:05:29,730 --> 00:05:32,450
The eyes might see
infrared, or ultraviolet.

104
00:05:32,950 --> 00:05:35,010
There might be three
of them, or a hundred.

105
00:05:35,371 --> 00:05:39,211
But using two eyes a little apart
is a cheap way to judge distance.

106
00:05:40,171 --> 00:05:45,411
It's one reason many hunters, like cats
and owls, have eyes facing forward.

107
00:05:45,987 --> 00:05:48,407
And now something almost unsettling.

108
00:05:48,812 --> 00:05:50,372
Put the eyes at the front.

109
00:05:50,632 --> 00:05:54,912
Put the mouth and the nose there too,
because that's where the food goes in.

110
00:05:55,438 --> 00:05:59,698
Add a social species that needs
to understand each other's moods.

111
00:06:00,099 --> 00:06:01,299
What do you get?

112
00:06:01,715 --> 00:06:04,455
Something that looks an
awful lot like a face.

113
00:06:05,046 --> 00:06:06,966
Not because aliens copied us.

114
00:06:07,046 --> 00:06:11,106
Because social animals face
the same communication problem.

115
00:06:13,686 --> 00:06:18,866
In 1982, a palaeontologist named Dale
Russell tried a thought experiment.

116
00:06:19,285 --> 00:06:21,985
What if the dinosaurs had never died out?

117
00:06:22,423 --> 00:06:26,383
He took a small, clever dinosaur,
a relative of Troodon, and

118
00:06:26,613 --> 00:06:29,943
imagined it evolving for
another sixty-six million years.

119
00:06:30,605 --> 00:06:32,205
The result stood upright.

120
00:06:32,865 --> 00:06:36,805
It had a big head, forward-facing
eyes, and hands with three fingers.

121
00:06:37,487 --> 00:06:39,527
He called it the dinosauroid.

122
00:06:39,885 --> 00:06:42,345
And many scientists criticised it.

123
00:06:42,765 --> 00:06:44,745
They said it was far too human.

124
00:06:45,365 --> 00:06:48,005
Basically, a person in a dinosaur costume.

125
00:06:48,932 --> 00:06:50,612
And that's the real debate.

126
00:06:51,524 --> 00:06:55,104
The biologist Stephen Jay Gould
argued, if you rewound the tape

127
00:06:55,334 --> 00:06:58,764
of life and played it again,
you'd almost never get humans.

128
00:06:59,535 --> 00:07:02,995
Others, like Simon Conway
Morris, argue the opposite.

129
00:07:03,855 --> 00:07:07,715
Convergence is so common that
something like us was likely to appear.

130
00:07:08,422 --> 00:07:10,042
They might both be right.

131
00:07:12,212 --> 00:07:14,092
The details wouldn't repeat.

132
00:07:14,692 --> 00:07:16,792
Gravity alone would change everything.

133
00:07:17,237 --> 00:07:20,337
On a heavy world, an alien
might be short and massive.

134
00:07:20,997 --> 00:07:22,997
On a light one, tall and slender.

135
00:07:23,649 --> 00:07:28,709
It might have six limbs, four eyes and
an exoskeleton, and look nothing like us.

136
00:07:28,928 --> 00:07:32,228
And still have a front,
something to process information,

137
00:07:32,388 --> 00:07:35,168
something to move with, and
something to build with.

138
00:07:35,701 --> 00:07:38,181
Human in architecture, not in appearance.

139
00:07:40,941 --> 00:07:43,501
And that might be the
answer to both questions.

140
00:07:44,301 --> 00:07:47,141
Life in the universe
could be wildly creative.

141
00:07:48,021 --> 00:07:51,881
Things without heads, without bodies,
without anything we'd call a face.

142
00:07:52,391 --> 00:07:54,631
But the aliens that build spaceships may

143
00:07:54,861 --> 00:07:57,331
have had to solve the
same problems we did.

144
00:07:57,685 --> 00:08:01,405
Give life a different world, and you
might get something unimaginable.

145
00:08:02,346 --> 00:08:05,026
Give it the same physics
and the same problems,

146
00:08:05,186 --> 00:08:07,546
and it might find some
of the same solutions.

147
00:08:08,330 --> 00:08:12,150
Maybe the universe doesn't have
one blueprint for intelligent life.

148
00:08:12,778 --> 00:08:15,668
Maybe it just has a
surprisingly small toolbox.
